# Copy to `.env.local`. Never commit `.env.local`.
#
# Every variable here is read in exactly one place — src/platform/config/ — which
# validates it with zod and refuses to start the server on anything missing or
# malformed. A lint rule keeps `process.env` out of the rest of the app.

# --- Environment -------------------------------------------------------------
# One of: local, development, staging, production. The same four names the backend
# (APP_ENV) and the player app (EXPO_PUBLIC_ENV) use — no synonyms, no `dev`, no `prod`.
#
# ONLY `local` RUNS TODAY. `development` and `staging` are defined and validated but
# NOT provisioned — no domain is registered — and `production` does not exist yet.
# Selecting one is a startup failure rather than a silent fallback: the scheme rule
# below requires https:// there, and the only reachable backend is an http:// one.
#
# SERVER-ONLY, and deliberately not mirrored into a NEXT_PUBLIC_ twin. The environment
# badge is rendered by a Server Component that reads this value, so there is exactly one
# copy of it and nothing to drift out of step.
#
# This is NOT `NODE_ENV`. That has two values and describes how the app was compiled;
# one production build serves staging and production alike. Nothing reads it.
APP_ENV=local

# --- URLs --------------------------------------------------------------------
# The scheme is part of the contract, not a convention:
#
#   local        http://     a laptop
#   development  https://    deployed, reached over TLS
#   staging      https://
#   production   https://
#
# SERVER-ONLY. Read by BFF route handlers (src/app/api/*) and Server Components.
# It is deliberately NOT prefixed NEXT_PUBLIC_ — the browser must never learn the
# FastAPI origin, and must never call it directly. See docs/adr/0007.
BACKEND_API_URL=http://localhost:8000

# Safe to expose — this is the club app's own public origin, which is the origin
# serving the page. Validated for the browser by src/platform/config/public.ts, and
# checked against the scheme rule above at server start.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=http://localhost:3000

# --- Session -----------------------------------------------------------------
# The name of the httpOnly session cookie the BFF writes. Letters, digits and
# !#$%&'*+-.^_`|~ only: a name with a space or a separator produces a Set-Cookie
# header browsers silently drop, which presents as "login does nothing".
SESSION_COOKIE_NAME=rakieta_club_session

# There is NO SESSION_COOKIE_SECURE. The Secure flag is DERIVED from APP_ENV —
# false only in `local`, true in every deployed environment — because an independently
# settable flag is a session transmitted in clear that looks deliberate. If the variable
# is still present in your environment the server refuses to start, rather than ignoring
# it and leaving you believing a security control is in effect that nothing reads.
